From: apklayer <noreply@apklayer.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] sigaction reports SIG_DFL, not the fatal-signal stub, as the old action of SIGSEGV/SIGILL/SIGBUS/SIGFPE

InitGuestSignalHandling claims SIGSEGV, SIGILL, SIGBUS and SIGFPE with kFatalGuestHandlerStub, the address of a host variable (libberberis_arm64's data), so that
HandleHostSignal runs first. rt_sigaction then handed that address to the guest as the previous action of a signal the guest never touched. A crash handler that
chains to the previous action (Gecko's fatal-signal handler at libxul+0x6a4fe50, Breakpad, Unity) branched to it: berberis_HandleNoExec ("Trying to execute
non-executable code at ...8460"), a new SIGSEGV from inside the handler, for ever (100% CPU, no crash). The old action now reads SIG_DFL with no flags, as the kernel's
would. Regression: tests/apps/hello-arm64 (oldHandlers). Module libberberis_arm64.

diff --git a/guest_os_primitives/guest_signal_handling.cc b/guest_os_primitives/guest_signal_handling.cc
--- a/guest_os_primitives/guest_signal_handling.cc
+++ b/guest_os_primitives/guest_signal_handling.cc
@@ -665,7 +665,16 @@
 
   std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(*GetSignalActionsGuardMutex());
   GuestSignalAction& action = GetCurrentGuestThread()->GetSignalActionsTable()->at(signal - 1);
-  return action.Change(signal, act, HandleHostSignal, old_act, error);
+  bool changed = action.Change(signal, act, HandleHostSignal, old_act, error);
+  // apklayer patch: the fatal-signal stub claims SIGSEGV/SIGILL/SIGBUS/SIGFPE, but it is the address of a host variable, not guest code: a crash
+  // handler that chains to the old action (Gecko's, Breakpad's) would jump to it and loop in berberis_HandleNoExec for ever. The guest has
+  // not installed a handler: report SIG_DFL, as the kernel does.
+  if (changed && old_act && old_act->guest_sa_sigaction == kFatalGuestHandlerStub) {
+    old_act->guest_sa_sigaction = Guest_SIG_DFL;
+    old_act->sa_flags = 0;
+    old_act->sa_mask = {};
+  }
+  return changed;
 }
 
 // region digitalis
